 },
 {
  "id": "L23/p3",
  "lesson": "L23",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>A two-layer attention-only transformer is trained on sequences made of a random block of tokens followed by the same block again. How does its loss on the second copy compare with the first?</p>",
  "answer": "c",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>About the same: the tokens are random, so nothing is predictable.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>Somewhat lower, from learning which tokens are common.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>Far lower, close to zero once training has gone on long enough: an induction circuit looks up each token's earlier occurrence and copies what followed it.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>Higher, because the model has to remember the first copy.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>The first copy is random, so its loss stays near <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln V</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">ln</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span></span></span></span> for a vocabulary of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span></span></span></span> tokens. The second copy is fully predictable from the first, and the network learns a previous-token head and an induction head that together look up and copy. The drop is abrupt during training. The capstone trains exactly this model and locates the two heads.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L23/p4",
  "lesson": "L23",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>A linear probe is trained on 100 examples of 200-dimensional random features with random labels. What training accuracy does it reach?</p>",
  "answer": "a",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>100%: with more dimensions than examples, some hyperplane separates any labelling.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>About 50%, since the labels are random.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>About 75%, half way between.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>It cannot be trained at all.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>With <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d \\ge n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8304em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≥</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span> points in general position can be separated in any way, so the probe fits the noise perfectly and is at chance on new data. Training accuracy measures the probe's capacity, not what the features contain. Report held-out accuracy, and compare with random features or a control task of the same size.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L23/concept1",
  "lesson": "L23",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why does a benchmark score need a confidence interval?",
  "answer": "The items are a sample from the tasks the benchmark stands for, so the score is an average with a standard error of about sqrt(p(1 - p)/n) over items.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L23/concept2",
  "lesson": "L23",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why compare two models item by item?",
  "answer": "Models find the same items hard, so their per-item scores are positively correlated and the variance of the paired difference is smaller than the sum of the separate variances.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L23/concept3",
  "lesson": "L23",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What does a linear probe show?",
  "answer": "That a property can be read linearly from the activations on held-out inputs; not that the model uses it, which needs a control task or random baseline and an intervention.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L23/concept4",
  "lesson": "L23",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is superposition?",
  "answer": "Representing more features than dimensions as nearly orthogonal directions, which works when features are sparse, at the cost of small interference between them.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L23/concept5",
  "lesson": "L23",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What does a sparse autoencoder optimize?",
  "answer": "Reconstruction error plus an L1 penalty on its latent activations, with unit-norm decoder rows, so that each input is explained by a few feature directions.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L23/concept6",
  "lesson": "L23",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What does activation patching measure?",
  "answer": "The fraction of the output difference between a clean and a corrupted input that is restored when one component's activation in the corrupted run is replaced by its clean value.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L23/concept7",
  "lesson": "L23",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "How does an induction head work?",
  "answer": "A previous-token head writes the identity of the preceding token into each position; the induction head's query for the current token matches that key at the position after its earlier occurrence and copies the token there.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
